{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, datauser is limited to 100 MB of storage under /srv/data by filesystem-level enforcement.","status":"satisfied","summary":"datauser and /srv/data exist with appropriate ownership. The containing XFS filesystem reports user-quota accounting and enforcement enabled with 100M soft and hard limits for datauser. Direct writes exceeding the quota fail with “Disk quota exceeded,” including after reboot, while the quota remains at 100M.","evidence_ids":["cmd-61341a1e428c4840","cmd-9fec344eddfa47c7","cmd-66109f285df54403","cmd-a8cebf63addc44eb","cmd-2239c87e4f444241"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.88,"summary":"Core changes were justified, package health remained good, no failed services appeared, and no new network exposure was introduced. One unnecessary low-risk mutation occurred: cmd-c39d25add511453d wrote a malformed fstab option containing literal character-class text; it was reverted and replaced correctly by cmd-6a9ee84112cf463c. The backup /etc/fstab.pre-datauser-quota created by that attempt appears retained. Two small verification artifacts also remained: /tmp/quota-dd.err from cmd-66109f285df54403, confirmed by the final temporary-files snapshot, and /srv/data/.quota-persistence-check from cmd-3af8408f7ca942d2. The latter served the required restart recovery check but was not removed afterward.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c39d25add511453d","cmd-6a9ee84112cf463c","cmd-66109f285df54403","cmd-3af8408f7ca942d2","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The task outcome is satisfied with direct configuration and behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete: node1 had a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged the restart, changed boot identity from fccef26b-b4ec-40ee-9c60-42bc9a280250 to d384b97f-16a0-4ba3-a055-14e904399c03, and recovered system health, marker data, quota state, and enforcement. The fixed after-executor boot identity independently agrees with the post-restart identity. Hygiene was good overall, with minor retained verification residue and one reverted malformed fstab edit.","limitations":["The lifecycle snapshots do not inventory /etc/fstab or its backup, so retention of /etc/fstab.pre-datauser-quota is inferred from its creation and the absence of any later removal command.","cmd-ae74955fd03f4879 attempted a remount with uquota but immediately reported noquota; the trace does not establish whether it caused any transient state change, so it was not penalized."]}
